A||||8||loafer87gt||Engine out the top or Bottom?||||1||
Z||000000||loafer87gt||08-03-2000||12:51 PM||||About to pull my old 2.8 and am curious as to what is the quickest and easiest way to pull and reinstall the engine. Also, is there any web pages dedicated to this topic, or does anyone know what issue of FOCOA mags covers the subject? I would like to get any info I could find to pass along to my buddy whos doing the mechanical work for me. He said the book time for pulling the engine and reinstalling it, reconnecting AC and accessories and is only 8.5 hours. This sounded awfully low to me!||||reg||1
Z||000001||Xtreme Fiero's||08-03-2000||01:09 PM||||It is easy to do if you have a car lift un hook all the lines, wires and the throttle, cruise and shift linkage. That will take about an hour or two, Lift the car enough to put the rear sub frame on a pallet, dolly or blocks. Then remove the rear wheels and unbolt the four cradle bolts (BE SURE THE CAR & CRADLE ARE SUPORTED PROPERLY!!!) slowly lift the car about 10 to 12 inches and un hook the wires you forgot then finish lifting he car all this will take about 3-4 hours so far, no the engine/Trans and rear suspension are out and free to work on do what you need done or want done while the engine is out (CHANGE THE PLUGS NOW! and check the front manifols) is all goes back the way it came apart i might take 2 peopple to get the front cradle bolts back in. 8.5 hours is about right more like 6.5-7 hours they throw that extra hour and a half for the mecanic to yell and scream at the car LOL. I hope i helped yo uout some||||reg||1
Z||000002||mshill||08-03-2000||01:26 PM||||From the bottom.  You need two good floor jacks, a 4x4, and a pallet with wheels on it  or a pair of furniture dollys.  I have done it once and I think I could have it out in about 4 hours the second time.  It actually goes back together faster than it comes apart.  ||||reg||1
Z||000003||prof bobo||08-03-2000||02:27 PM||||Here's how I do it, you needn't discharge your A/C, and, by leaving the struts attached to the frame, you won't have to get a wheel alignment.  Takes me 'bout 3-4 hours, but I've done this over 1/2 dozen times . . .<P><BR>Check off each item as done: <P>     Raise front of car 6-8" (Put on ramps), remove trunk side louvre covers. <BR>     Drain oil & coolant. <BR>     Disconnect battery, remove battery and plastic protector (13mm & 9/32"). <BR>     Remove trunk lid ground strap & lite connector, then lid (13mm). <BR>     Remove rear console, disconnect ECM harness from ECM, push harness through firewall to engine. <BR>     Disconnect all engine ground straps, battery harness connector block & fusible links (10mm & 1/4"). <BR>     Disconnect upper radiator hose at thermostat, heater hoses at water pump. <BR>     Remove rear torque reaction strut bolt, tip up strut (15mm). <BR>     Remove intake flex duct and PCV elbow, disconnect air temp sensor, remove air filter. <BR>     Remove fuel pump relay (leave connected to harness) (1/4"). <BR>     Disconnect throttle cable and cruise control cable (15mm). <BR>     Disconnect vacuum hoses, label as necessary. <BR>     Disconnect fuel lines at engine. <BR>     Disconnect clutch slave cylinder, shifter cables w/support 'S' bracket (13mm & 15mm). <BR>     Remove rear trunk heat shield (1/4"). <BR>     Raise rear of car, put on blocks. <BR>     Disconnect lower radiator hoses at pipes. <BR>     Remove front trunk heat shield (10mm). <BR>     Disconnect A/C wiring, remove A/C compressor don't discharge, support out of way (15mm). <BR>     Remove rear wheels, remove fender splash guards. <BR>     Disconnect main parking brake cable from cradle, each wheel cable (save spring). <BR>     Remove and suspend brake calipers w/hoses intact (detach brake hose clamps at strut), remove rotors (T-45 &<BR>     10mm). <BR>     Remove upper strut nuts and side louvre brackets. <BR>     Loosen, but don't remove cradle bolts. Cradle will fall otherwise! (16mm & 17mm) <BR>     Lower car till cradle is resting on dolly. <BR>     Remove cradle bolts. <BR>     Raise car (jack and block, jack and block) about 3', until cradle can be rolled out. Watch for interference! <BR>     Disconnect O2 sensor, remove exhaust Y-pipe, EGR valve and heat shield (12mm & 10mm). <BR>     Disconnect radiator intermediate pipe (10mm & 15mm). <BR>     Remove starter, remove sheet metal clutch cover (15mm & 10mm). <BR>     Disconnect speedometer sensor. <BR>     Disconnect engine mounts (15mm) <BR>     Disconnect transaxle, pry only at cast boss at upper rear (18mm). <BR>     Lift engine. <P>  <BR>  Protect CV joint boots! <BR>  Keep axles supported while on cradle so inner CV joints don't separate. <BR>  Discard old exhaust component bolts. ||||reg||1
Z||000004||Xtreme Fiero's||08-03-2000||02:38 PM||||By removing the struts from the body you won't need a alighment, Just hang the brake calapers and lines out of the way with a old coat hanger.||||reg||5
Z||000005||loafer87gt||08-03-2000||03:06 PM||||Great info guys! Thanks a lot! I'll post some pics of the new engine when its installed. I'm installing a 3.0 V6 engine built by RSM motorsports that is going to be putting down about 190 HP. I can't wait to take it out to the track and see what sort of times I'm running. Once agin, thanks.||||reg||1
